{"name":"Aged Jade","hex":"#6c6956","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aged","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Old.","Having the age of."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Old people, collectively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aged"},{"term":"jade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A semiprecious stone, either nephrite or jadeite, generally green or white in color, often used for carving figurines.","A bright shade of slightly bluish or greyish green, typical of polished jade stones."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish shade of green, typical of jade stones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ageing","blocks":[{"text":"Ageing is the process of becoming older. The term refers mainly to humans, many other animals, and fungi; whereas for example, bacteria, perennial plants and some simple animals are potentially biologically immortal. In a broader sense, ageing can refer to single cells within an organism which have ceased dividing, or to the population of a species."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ageing"},{"title":"Jade","blocks":[{"text":"Jade is an umbrella term for two different types of decorative rocks used for jewelry or ornaments. Jade is often referred to by either of two different silicate mineral names: nephrite, or jadeite. Nephrite is typically green, although it may be yellow, white or black. Jadeite varies from white or near-colorless, through various shades of green, to lavender, yellow, orange, brown and black. Rarely it may be blue. Both of these names refer to their use as gemstones, and each has a mineralogically more specific name. Both the amphibole jade (nephrite) and pyroxene jade are mineral aggregates (rocks) rather than mineral species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Jadestein.jpg/330px-Jadestein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jade"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViews_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/500px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/1280px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/500px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Immortal Hydra, a relative of the jellyfish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydras_(8).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Senescence.JPG/500px-Senescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig